    {
      "index": 3,
      "id": "motivation",
      "heading": "Motivation",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "chunk_index": 1,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c01",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c1_diffusion_models_become_powerful_cla",
          "text": "Diffusion models have become a powerful class of generative models. They work by defining a forward process that gradually destroys data into Gaussian noise, then learning a reverse process that denoises noise back into realistic samples, without ever estimating a value function.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "diffusion",
            "models",
            "become",
            "powerful",
            "class",
            "generative",
            "models",
            "they",
            "work",
            "defining"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 2,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c02",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c2_authors_ask_simple_question_what",
          "text": "The authors ask a simple question: what if we treat goal states as the data distribution we want to model?",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "authors",
            "ask",
            "simple",
            "question",
            "what",
            "treat",
            "goal",
            "states",
            "distribution",
            "want"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 3,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c03",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c3_diffusion_noise_walks_away_manifold",
          "text": "In a diffusion model, noise walks away from the data manifold; in goal-conditioned reinforcement learning, we can construct trajectories that walk away from potential goals.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "diffusion",
            "noise",
            "walks",
            "away",
            "manifold",
            "goal-conditioned",
            "reinforcement",
            "learning",
            "construct",
            "trajectories"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 4,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c04",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c4_learning_reverse_those_deviations_di",
          "text": "Learning to reverse those deviations is directly analogous to learning the score function, and it sidesteps the value-estimation problems that plague offline methods.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "learning",
            "reverse",
            "those",
            "deviations",
            "directly",
            "analogous",
            "learning",
            "score",
            "function",
